          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Not always, but usually it is. The retrieved sources explain the coding structure. They do not cover payer policies or appeal rules, so I can't say whether this particular request is valid.\n\n**Why the request is often reasonable.** In ICD-10-CM, a three-character code is usually a category heading. Characters four to six add detail such as cause, site, severity, or side of the body, and some categories also use a seventh character for the episode of care. Most categories need those extra characters before they count as complete (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions; coding-hypertension-i10). Asthma (J45) is one example. The sources say it is never used at three characters (coding-asthma-j45).\n\n**When the request may not fit.** Some three-character codes are complete on their own. Essential hypertension, I10, is one. The sources say a request to \"add more specificity\" to I10 is one the classification cannot satisfy (coding-hypertension-i10). If the code on your claim is complete, the payer's request may rest on a mistake or a different code set.\n\n**What to check.**\n- Whether the code you submitted is a category that has subcodes.\n- Whether your medical record actually states the extra detail. Coders should choose a more specific code only when the documentation supports it, not guess at it. If the record is silent, the fix is usually a clarification from the treating clinician.\n- The payer's own coding policy and the reason printed on the denial or request notice. The payer's rules decide what counts as complete for that claim.\n\n**What I could not cover.** The sources do not describe how payers decide when extra characters are required, what deadlines apply, or how to appeal. Your billing office or a certified medical coder can review the claim and the record. They can also tell you whether an appeal is worth filing.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
